        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"A man in winter clothing standing in the middle of a city street while holding a gun.\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"Image: Columbia Pictures\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/DF-04181_r.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When Paul W.S. Anderson's <em>Resident Evil </em>hit theaters in 2002, video game movies were largely seen as a niche. Films like <em>Mortal Kombat</em> and <em>Tomb Raider</em> had proven that big-screen game adaptations could be successful, but the commercial and critical failure of projects like the 1993 <em>Super Mario Bros.</em> still cast a long shadow. And while Anderson's vision of Raccoon City might have captivated audiences on the whole, many hardcore fans of Capcom's games were left disappointed by the way the original movies wildly deviated from the source material.</p>",
